why 3 1/2 DOUBLING In the D major chord (III in B minor) Bach doubles the third, F♯. F♯ is the dominant of the home key, a strong note to double.
why 2 1/4 SPACING The bass falls to a low F♯ for the dominant, two octaves under the tenor's F♯, which gives the chord a deep, full sound.
why 6 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All four voices fall into the B minor chord: the seventh E falls to D and the bass to the tonic, so every pull points down.
why 2 3/8 SPACING The bass keeps its low F♯ while the tenor passes down to E. The wide gap lasts only for this dominant.
why 5 3/4 PROGRESSION The soprano climbs C♯–D–E and falls back, and the chords under it decorate one long approach to the cadence. The V here is passing; the cadential V comes in bar 6.
why 6 1/4 SEVENTH The tenor's F♯ over the G chord is the root of the coming dominant, sounding early. It leaps to C♯, the fifth of V, instead of falling.
why 6 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and bass move D–C♯ and G–F♯ a fifth apart. The bass slips down to E between them, and for Bach that is enough to break up the fifths; strict exercises still count them.
why 6 1/4 LEAPS The tenor steps G–F♯ and leaps down to C♯, the fifth of V. G and C♯ belong to different chords, so the tritone is not heard as one interval.
why 7 1/8 SPACING The tenor drops an octave to a low E and then climbs F♯–G♯–A. The drop gives it room to rise by step through the next chords.
why 7 1/8 CROSSING The tenor's octave drop takes it below the bass for an eighth. The bass is falling to D♯ at the same moment, so the order is back at once.
why 7 1/4 SPACING The gap comes from the tenor's octave drop and closes again as the tenor climbs F♯–G♯–A.
why 7 5/8 DOUBLING Over the bass's held F♯ the upper voices pass through a diminished chord, E♯–G♯–B, on a weak eighth. It is a passing chord over a held bass, so the ⁶₄ doubling rule does not apply.
why 7 5/8 SIX_FOUR This is a passing chord over the held bass F♯, a pedal ⁶₄ in all but name, on a weak eighth.
why 9 1/2 DOUBLING The melody leaps up to its high F♯ while the alto steps G–F♯. Again F♯, the home key's dominant note, is doubled: a strong note even as the third of III.
why 10 3/4 SPACING On the last dominant the bass drops an octave to a low F♯ for weight at the cadence, while the tenor rises to E, the seventh. The deep bass is deliberate.
why 10 3/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs the dominant seventh chord, A♯–C♯–E, to reach the seventh E, which then falls to D♯. An arpeggio of V⁷ always contains this tritone.
